{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat _commentary/v9/prompts/luna_worker.md && printf '\\\\n--- WORKLIST ---\\\\n' && cat _commentary/v9/luna/work/29_38/W1_branches_10.md\"","aggregated_output":"# V9 Luna lane — worklist judge\n\nYou judge one worklist of items for one Quranic ayah. You do not write prose. Your records are the only\nthing a later writer sees, so a finding you skip is lost.\n\n## Read (whole files, nothing skipped)\n\n1. `context.md` in the work directory: the focus ayah with its words and anchor translation, the Fatiha\n   (recited in every salah), and the whole host surah.\n2. Your worklist file (named in the launch message). Shell output is truncated on long reads: read it in\n   consecutive chunks (`sed -n '1,150p'`, `sed -n '151,300p'`, …) until the last line. The file header\n   lists its first and last item ids; confirm you reached the last one.\n\n## Judge every item\n\nAn item proposes that something activates a latent sense in the focus ayah: a dictionary branch with its\nimage partners (R…B…), a word's Quranic usage (R….U), a precomputed hypothesis (H…), a lead (L…), or\nanother ayah (G…). The canonical meaning is known; you look for what is latent.\n\n- **reading** — two keys hold: (1) a sense the dictionary gives (or the Quranic usage shows), and (2) an\n  independent trigger — an actual word in the focus ayah, its surah, the Fatiha or the other ayah — that\n  activates it. Rare, surprising and multi-step links are welcome when both keys hold. Polysemy is allowed:\n  a rare branch (an eye disease, a mountain, kohl) can be heard beside the plain sense when a trigger\n  calls it.\n- **note** — real but one key only: a striking branch with a weak trigger, or a strong trigger for a\n  sense the dictionary only half gives.\n- **none** — with a code: `no-trigger` (a branch nothing calls), `noise` (the pairing is accidental),\n  `canonical` (only the plain sense, nothing latent), `wrong` (the listed link misreads the Arabic), or\n  `same-as:<id>` (the same point as an earlier record).\n\nFor inter-ayah items (G…) the review labels are earlier judgements, not decisions: judge whether the\nother ayah's actual words activate or move something in the focus ayah. For HFT items (H…) check each\ntrace step against the Arabic. An echo root is a sound-family candidate, never the word's identity.\n\nBeyond the list: when reading shows you a link no item proposes (a word in the surah that calls a\nbranch, two items that join into one image), add a record with id `X<n>` (X1, X2, …).\n\n## Numbered evidence lines\n\nBranch and usage items (R…) list their evidence as numbered lines `[1] … [n]` (the heading says\n`lines: n`). Judge every line on its own: does this partner, concept path, Fatiha pair or bridge activate\nthe branch? Give the item a `lines` string with exactly one code per line, in order: `-` nothing,\n`n` note, `r` reading. Every `n` or `r` line gets its own full record with id `<item>.<k>` (the line's\nnumber). A concept line's value is its path: say which concept (night, eye, road…) carries the image to\nwhich word. A branch whose own ayah activates it beyond the listed lines also gets a verdict on the item\nrecord itself; an item with `lines: 0` always needs a verdict.\n\n## Records\n\nAppend one JSON object per line to `records/<worklist stem>.jsonl` in the work directory (for\n`W1_branches_3.md` → `records/W1_branches_3.jsonl`). Write in batches of about ten items as you go, never\nall at the end. Fields:\n\n```\n{\"id\": \"R03.B002\", \"verdict\": \"reading\", \"focus_ar\": \"مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\", \"branch\": \"س ك ن B002\",\n \"trigger_ar\": \"بَيْتًا\", \"trigger_ref\": \"29:41\",\n \"before\": \"their dwellings (plain)\", \"after\": \"the image the reading hears\",\n \"reason\": \"which words do what: the specific Arabic words, the branch image, and how the trigger calls it\",\n \"code\": \"\"}\n{\"id\": \"R03.B007\", \"lines\": \"--------\", \"verdict\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-trigger\"}\n{\"id\": \"R10.B001\", \"lines\": \"r-------r------\"}\n{\"id\": \"R10.B001.1\", \"verdict\": \"reading\", \"focus_ar\": \"مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\", \"branch\": \"ب ص ر B001\", …}\n{\"id\": \"R10.B001.9\", \"verdict\": \"reading\", …}\n```\n\n- `focus_ar`: the word of the focus ayah, copied from it. `trigger_ar`: the activating word(s), copied\n  from the ayah in `trigger_ref` (one `S:A`; for a trigger inside the focus ayah use the focus reference).\n- `reason`: specific to this item — name the Arabic words and the image. Never reuse a sentence; if two\n  items make one point, keep the better one and mark the other `same-as`.\n- English or Turkish, short sentences. Readings need at least two sentences of substance.\n\n## Finish\n\nRun `python3 _commentary/v9/luna/check_records.py <work dir> <worklist name>` from the repository root and\nfix every problem it reports (missing ids, empty fields, Arabic not found in the cited ayah, repeated\nsentences). Stop when it prints `ok`.\n\n--- WORKLIST ---\n# W1_branches_10.md — 8 items: R10.U … R10.B007\n\nRoots in this package:\n- R01 ع و د (root_001058) — identity root of وَعَادًا (w1)\n- R02 ب ي ن (root_000170) — identity root of تَّبَيَّنَ (w4)\n- R03 س ك ن (root_000726) — identity root of مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ (w7)\n- R04 ز ي ن (root_000660) — identity root of وَزَيَّنَ (w8)\n- R05 ش ط ن (root_000796) — identity root of ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ (w10)\n- R06 ع م ل (root_001046) — identity root of أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ (w11)\n- R07 ص د د (root_000848) — identity root of فَصَدَّهُمْ (w12)\n- R08 س ب ل (root_000672) — identity root of ٱلسَّبِيلِ (w14)\n- R09 ك و ن (root_001332) — identity root of وَكَانُوا۟ (w15)\n- R10 ب ص ر (root_000121) — identity root of مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ (w16)\n- R11 ECHO ع د د (root_000989) — for وَعَادًا (w1): withheld observed target; not identity\n### R10.U — usage of ب ص ر (ب ص ر (root_000121) — identity root of مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ (w16)) — lines: 9\nBu kök çok dallıdır: gözle görme ve iç kavrayış gibi algı temelli dalların yanında açıklık, kan izi, koruyucu savaş gereci, kalın kenar-ek yeri ve yumuşak parlak taş anlamları bulunur. Dallar eş adlı ve kısmen imgesel bağlantılar taşısa da her biri kendi madde, eylem veya kavrayış alanında sınırlandırılmalıdır.\nمُسْتَبْصِرِينَ (w16) — root ب ص ر, lemma مُسْتَبْصِرِين\n[1] in surah 29: 29:38 مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\n[2] Quran lemmas: بَصِير 51, بَصَر 48, أَبْصَرَ 29, بَصِيرَة 7, مُبْصِر 4, مُبْصِرَة 3, بَصُرَتْ 3, يُبَصَّرُ 1, مُسْتَبْصِرِين 1, تَبْصِرَة 1\n[3] **hapax**: lemma مُسْتَبْصِرِين occurs only here.\n[4] near-synonym contrast (nearest branches of other roots):\n[5] ر ء ي B001 gözle ya da içsel kavrayışla görme / رؤية العين والبصيرة — Quran 328 occ.: 2:55, 2:73, 2:128, 2:144, 2:165, 2:166, 2:167, 2:243\n[6] ع ي ن B001 gören göz / العين الناظرة — Quran 65 occ.: 2:60, 3:13, 5:45, 5:83, 7:116, 7:160, 7:179, 7:195\n[7] ن ظ ر B001 bakıp inceleme / توجيه البصر أو البصيرة لإدراك الشيء — Quran 129 occ.: 2:50, 2:55, 2:69, 2:104, 2:162, 2:210, 2:259, 2:280\n[8] ث ب ت B006 kanıtla belirleme, hükme bağlama veya sözle ileri sürme / الثبت والحجة والإثبات — Quran 18 occ.: 2:250, 2:265, 3:147, 4:66, 8:11, 8:12, 8:30, 8:45\n[9] ر ء ي B002 düşünüp bir görüşe varma / رأي القلب والتفكر — Quran 328 occ.: 2:55, 2:73, 2:128, 2:144, 2:165, 2:166, 2:167, 2:243\n\n### R10.B001 — ب ص ر B001 — lines: 16\ndictionary: **B001** gözle görme | إبصار العين | Gözle görme alanı: görme duyusu, bakan göz organı, bir şeyi gözle görmek ve gözün görmeye açılması. Dikkatle dikilerek bakma veya yavrunun gözünün açılması gibi kullanımlar bu çekirdeğe bağlı özel gerçekleşmelerdir. | facets: Görme duyusu, bakan göz organı ve bir şeyi gözle görme eylemi çekirdeği oluşturur. / Görme yetisine sahip kişi kör olanın karşıtı olarak adlandırılır. / Dikkatle ve keskin biçimde bakma, görme çekirdeğine bağlı kalıplaşmış bir kullanımdır. / Yavrunun gözünün açılıp görmeye hazır hale gelmesi de gör… | not: لا يدخل فيه علم القلب والبصيرة الدينية ولا الح